# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Task Earn Hub — environment variables (NAMES ONLY — no secret values)
#
# LOCAL DEVELOPMENT: copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in the values:
#   macOS / Linux:        cp .env.example .env.local
#   Windows (cmd):        copy .env.example .env.local
#   Windows (PowerShell): Copy-Item .env.example .env.local
# (`.env` works too — Next.js loads both; `.env.local` is git-ignored.)
#
# PRODUCTION (Vercel): copy the variable NAMES into
#   Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables
# and fill in the values from your own Supabase project dashboard.
# Never commit real values — `.env*` is git-ignored.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Local SQLite database used by the app's local data track AND as the
# always-readable source of truth for the data-backend switch
# (SystemSetting row "data_backend" = "local" | "supabase").
# NOTE — Prisma resolves relative SQLite paths against the prisma/ schema
# folder (its documented rule), so "../db/custom.db" points at the
# <project-root>/db/custom.db file in dev, CLI and the production server.
# Use a RELATIVE path (never an absolute machine-specific path).
#
# OPTIONAL at runtime: if this variable is missing, src/lib/db.ts falls back
# to the same bundled `file:../db/custom.db` automatically, so `npm run dev`
# works right after unzip. It is still REQUIRED for the Prisma CLI commands
# (`npm run db:push`, `npm run db:seed`) and recommended always.
DATABASE_URL=file:../db/custom.db

# Your Supabase project URL (Project Settings → API → Project URL).
# Required for secure authentication + password recovery emails.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Your Supabase service-role secret key (Project Settings → API keys).
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY — the app never exposes it to the browser.
# Required for: Supabase data track, admin operations, password recovery.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# OPTIONAL but recommended in production: a long random string used to sign
# session cookies. If unset, a built-in development fallback is used (fine for
# local development; generate a unique value for your live deployment, e.g.
# `openssl rand -hex 32`).
JWT_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — admin provisioning helper only (NOT read by the website itself)
#
# scripts/supabase-setup.ts uses these to install YOUR admin account in the
# local database + Supabase Auth. Leave empty if you never run that script.
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_NAME=

# OPTIONAL — debug scripts override (scripts/debug-inspect-packages.ts,
# scripts/fix-package-int-overflow.ts). Defaults to db/custom.db.
# DB_PATH=db/custom.db
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Supabase Auth URL Configuration — set these in the SUPABASE DASHBOARD
# (Authentication → URL Configuration), NOT as app env vars. Listed here so
# the required values are documented next to the keys they belong with.
#
#   SITE_URL           = https://YOUR-PRODUCTION-DOMAIN
#                        (e.g. https://your-app.vercel.app)
#   REDIRECT_URLS      = https://YOUR-PRODUCTION-DOMAIN/**
#                        (add http://localhost:3000/** too if you also run
#                        the app locally against the same Supabase project)
#
# Until SITE_URL points at your production domain, Supabase rewrites the
# password-recovery links to the old Site URL and emailed links will not open
# on your members' phones. See DEPLOYMENT.md §4.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
